column1:
Methods used for detecting mutations include:

Cytogenetics analysis, which involves examining the structure and number of chromosomes, is a traditional approach for identifying genetic changes. Additionally, various molecular techniques have been developed to detect mutations. These include:

FISH (fluorescence in situ hybridization), which uses fluorescent probes to detect specific DNA sequences;
PCR (polymerase chain reaction), a technique that amplifies specific DNA sequences;
RFLP (restriction fragment length polymorphism), which involves cutting DNA at specific sites and analyzing the resulting fragments;
DNA sequencing, which determines the order of nucleotides in a DNA molecule;
Southern blotting, a technique that uses DNA probes to detect specific DNA sequences;
Microarrays, which use small DNA fragments attached to a surface to detect specific DNA sequences;
Single-stranded conformational polymorphism (SSCP), which involves analyzing the conformation of single-stranded DNA;
RNAase cleavage, which uses enzymes to cleave RNA molecules;
Denaturating gradient gel electrophoresis (DGGE), which separates DNA fragments based on their melting points;
Protein truncation test, which detects mutations by analyzing the length of proteins.
